# Conditional guidance

Some of this project's guidance applies CONDITIONALLY. Each entry gives a
fragment's NAME and the PREMISE under which it applies. Load any your
context does not already carry.

Match against WHAT YOU ARE ABOUT TO DO -- the next action, not the whole
conversation behind you. Take each premise ON ITS OWN and ask whether it
describes that action; you are not picking the single best match, and
several premises often apply at once.

When it is a BORDERLINE call, include it. An unnecessary fragment costs a
little context; one you withhold is never learned to exist and cannot be
asked for.

Select on the premise, not on the name. Then call the ctxloom
`assemble_context` tool with the names you chose (the `bundles` argument)
and follow what it returns.

- ctxloom-project#fragments/commit-timeouts: You are about to run git commit, git merge or git push through the shell tool — directly, inside a script, or as the last step of a change. For anyone whose next command fires this repository's git hooks, however the moment is worded.
- ctxloom-project#fragments/config-hierarchy: You are adding or changing a flag, an environment variable, or a config key; working out which layer won when a setting resolved unexpectedly; or reading configuration to decide how something is declared. For anyone touching how this family of binaries is configured, however the moment is worded.
- ctxloom-project#fragments/dind-in-agent-cells: You are about to give an agent docker access, ask for docker-in-docker for a cell, or work out why an ordinary build, test or lint recipe fails inside an agent container. For anyone reaching for a container runtime from inside a cell, however the moment is worded. This is about whether the cell needs docker AT ALL; turn-gates is about which gate proves a change.
- ctxloom-project#fragments/fail-loud-launch: You are writing a check that can find something wrong; writing code that accepts a configuration value, a file, or any input from outside the program; deciding what an unknown, malformed, or missing value should resolve to; or choosing between refusing and carrying on with less. For anyone deciding how loudly a failure should surface, and where the set of acceptable values is declared. This one also governs ordinary resolution code, not only launch-time findings.
- ctxloom-project#fragments/green-is-not-passing: You have just written or changed a test; you are reading a gate result and deciding what it proves; or you are about to describe something as verified, working, or done. For anyone about to treat a green result as evidence, however that moment arises. More than one fragment may apply here; this one is about whether the evidence is real.
- ctxloom-project#fragments/turn-gates: You are writing or changing a test during a turn, and need it to survive the close-out mutation run; choosing which build, test, lint, or mutation command to run; interpreting an exit code or a suite's output; or deciding whether the gate you ran actually covers the claim you are making. For anyone deciding which command proves a change is done. This is about WHICH gate and how to read it, not about whether a passing test means anything.
- premised#fragments/sometimes: the agent is about to edit a signed bundle